<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.8.4: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02467</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.8.4: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>aacaaedd-9962-407d-987f-eca1b1528a30</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="8b80144d-2c30-45be-bd97-c425d2c93597" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--1a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="db3e5236-c2a2-49c7-804d-f43ce4d36a8b" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="1">
    <problem>
      <para>The quadratic equation \(x^2+7x+10=0\) is in the form of \(ax^2+bx+c=0\).</para>
      <para>What is the value of \(a\)?</para>
      <para>Enter the value of \(a\).</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The value of \(a\) is 1.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="acb22ad4-e36a-4c14-b252-8214e407a7d2" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="7">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the value of \(b\)?</para>
      <para> Enter the value of \(b\).</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The value of \(b\) is 7.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="78372383-13cd-404a-8b99-0cb68702154a" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="10">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the value of \(c\)?</para>
      <para> Enter the value of \(c\).</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The value of \(c\) is 10.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1d-->
  <exercise data-content-id="0d72bfb3-3bc6-42ee-8980-b3f3663afc70" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the squared term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square." data-solution-options="[&quot;You must always multiply both sides by 4 when completing the square.&quot;, &quot;If the value of \\(c\\) is positive and you multiply by 4, it will be easier to divide the \\(b\\) term by 3 before you square it.&quot;, &quot;Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the \\(b\\) term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.&quot;, &quot;Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the squared term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Examine the solution method that has been started below and use it to answer parts d and e.</para>
      <para>Original equation
        \(x^2+7x+10=0\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Subtract 10 from each side.
        \(x^2+7x=-10\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply each side by 4.
        \(4x^2+4(7x)=-4(10)\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\).
        \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +\_\_\_\_^2=\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\)
        \((2x+\_\_\_\_)^2=\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\)
        \(2x+\_\_\_\_= \pm \sqrt{\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)}\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\)
        \(2x=\_\_\_\_\pm \sqrt{\_\_\_\_^2-4(10)}\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - \(\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\)
        \(x= \_\_\_\_\)</para>
      <para>In Step 2, what might be a good reason for multiplying each side of the equation by 4?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Multiplying by 4 makes the coefficient of the squared term a perfect square, which makes it easier to complete the square.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1e-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a625e32e-f588-4181-a0ab-9075ea8737d1" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;-5&quot;, &quot;-2&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;-10&quot;, &quot;-5&quot;, &quot;-2&quot;, &quot;-4&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Complete the unfinished steps, then <emphasis>select two</emphasis> solutions.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The solutions are -5 and -2.</para>
      <para>Here are the unfinished steps:</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Rewrite \(4x^2\) as \((2x)^2\) and \(4(7x)\) as \(2(7)2x\).
        \((2x)^2+2(7)(2x) +(7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial.
        \((2x+7)^2=(7)^2-4(10)\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side.
        \(2x+7= \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Subtract 7 from both sides.
        \(2x=-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(10)}\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Solve.
        \(x=-5\), -2</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--1f-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1f83ae39-a00f-4bd9-8b72-550d1843c7a6" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Rather than evaluating at each step, the calculation is done all at once, at the end." data-solution-options="[&quot;In both forms of solving the equation, you need to list the values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) to determine the solutions.&quot;, &quot;Rather than evaluating at each step, the calculation is done all at once, at the end.&quot;, &quot;They are related because neither one can have a negative \\(c\\) value.&quot;, &quot;For this equation, the quadratic formula and completing the square are not the same and therefore not related.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Substitute the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) into the quadratic formula, \(x=\frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2- 4ac}}{2a}\), but do not evaluate any of the expressions. Explain how this expression is related to solving \(x^2+7x+10=0\) by completing the square.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Rather than evaluating at each step, the calculation is done all at once, at the end.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="f30fb96e-9c43-4d25-8acb-2c705204e8eb" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="1">
    <problem>
      <para>Consider the standard form of the equation \(x^2−39=0\).</para>
      <para>What is the value of \(a\)?</para>
      <para>Enter the value of \(a\).</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The value of \(a\) is 1.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="9310921f-1e37-4cb5-894d-00b995a9df75" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="0">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the value of \(b\)?</para>
      <para> Enter the value of \(b\).</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The value of \(b\) is 0.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="44da0935-583a-49f8-8fbf-42395ea81a40" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-39">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the value of \(c\)?</para>
      <para> Enter the value of \(c\).</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The value of \(c\) is -39.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit 9.8.1. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2d-->
  <exercise data-content-id="69305262-192e-44aa-bd03-412bc5c66070" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Yes" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Can you use the quadratic formula to solve this equation?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The quadratic formula works for all quadratic equations. Substituting the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) into the quadratic formula and evaluating the expression gives the solutions.
      </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 – 9.8.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2e-->
  <exercise data-content-id="61fb1551-01ea-42b2-ac7d-a9bb6e748245" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Yes, the solutions are \(\pm \sqrt{39}\)." data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes, the solutions are \\(\\pm 39\\).&quot;, &quot;No, there is no solution.&quot;, &quot;Yes, the solutions are \\(\\pm \\sqrt{39}\\).&quot;, &quot;No, this equation can only be solved by the quadratic formula.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Can you solve this equation using square roots?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Yes, adding 39 to both sides of the equation gives \(x^2=39\). The solutions are \(\pm \sqrt{39}\). </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 and 9.8.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="8f682d62-d494-473f-8d61-49e01216ec73" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="Find the square roots of each side. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then divide each side by \(2a\)." data-solution-options="[&quot;Subtract \\(b^2\\) from each side. Find the square roots of each side. Then divide each side by 2.&quot;, &quot;Find the square roots of each side. Subtract \\(b \\) from each side. Then divide each side by \\(2a\\).&quot;, &quot;Divide each side by 2. Subtract \\(b \\) from each side. Then find the square roots of each side.&quot;, &quot;Find the square roots of each side. Divide each side by 2. Subtract \\(b \\) from each side.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Clare is deriving the quadratic formula by solving \(ax^2+bx+c=0\) by completing the square.</para>
      <para>She arrived at this equation: \((2ax+b)^2=b^2−4ac\).</para>
      <para>Choose the best description of what she needs to do to finish solving for \(x\).</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! She needs to find the square roots of each side. Subtract \(b \) from each side. Then divide each side by \(2a\). </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para> Try again. Revisit activities 9.8.1 – 9.8.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div></section>
</content>
